1. Switching
Definition: Switching operates within a single Local Area Network (LAN) to facilitate communication between interconnected host devices (e.g., PCs, servers, printers).

OSI Layer: Layer 2 (Data Link Layer)
Addressing Mechanism: Media Access Control (MAC) Address
Data Unit: Frame

How It Works:

MAC Address Table (CAM Table): The switch builds and maintains a dynamically updated table mapping device MAC addresses to physical switch ports.

Unicast Delivery: Unlike a hub (which broadcasts data to all ports), a switch forwards incoming frames strictly to the destination port associated with the target MAC address.

Frame Processing: Reads Layer 2 source and destination MAC headers to direct local network traffic without inspecting IP data.

2. Routing
Definition: Routing connects distinct, separate networks (e.g., connecting a local LAN to an Internet Service Provider network or across different subnets).

OSI Layer: Layer 3 (Network Layer)
Addressing Mechanism: Internet Protocol (IP) Address
Data Unit: Packet

How It Works:

Routing Table Inspection: Routers maintain a routing table containing known network destinations, metrics, and next-hop gateways.

Path Determination: Upon receiving a packet, the router inspects the destination IP address and uses routing algorithms/protocols (e.g., OSPF, BGP, Static Routes) to determine the optimal outbound interface.

Packet Forwarding: Decapsulates Layer 2 headers, updates TTL (Time to Live), re-encapsulates the Layer 3 packet with new Layer 2 headers, and forwards it to the next-hop router.

3. Switching vs Routing
Network Scope:
Switching: Intra-network (Same LAN / Subnet)
Routing: Inter-network (Cross-LAN / WAN / Internet)

OSI Layer:
Switching: Layer 2 (Data Link)
Routing: Layer 3 (Network)

Addressing:
Switching: MAC Address
Routing: IP Address

PDU:
Switching: Frame
Routing: Packet

Primary Device:
Switching: L2 Network Switch
Routing: Router / L3 Switch

4. Firewalls
Definition: A firewall acts as a perimeter security control that analyzes incoming and outgoing network traffic against a defined set of security rules (Access Control Lists).

OSI Layer: Layers 3 (Network), 4 (Transport), and 7 (Application)
Core Function: Enforce Allow or Deny/Block policies on network connections.

Key Types and Inspection Mechanisms:

Stateless / Packet Filtering: Filters traffic based purely on static parameters (Source IP, Destination IP, Source Port, Destination Port, Protocol).

Stateful Inspection: Tracks the state of active network connections (connection state table). Automatically allows return traffic for legitimately established outbound requests.

Next-Generation Firewall (NGFW): Combines stateful inspection with deep packet inspection (DPI), Intrusion Prevention Systems (IPS), malware detection, and application-layer visibility (Layer 7).

5. Virtual Private Networks (VPNs)
Definition: A VPN constructs a secure, encrypted connection (tunnel) over an untrusted or public network (such as the Internet).

Primary Objectives: Data Confidentiality (Encryption), Integrity (Anti-tampering), and Authentication.

Key Concepts and Implementations:

Tunneling Protocols: Encapsulates network traffic within secure transport protocols (e.g., IPsec, OpenVPN, WireGuard).

Encryption Standards: Scrambles payload data using cryptographic ciphers (e.g., AES-256) so intercepted traffic remains unreadable to unauthorized third parties.

Common Architectures:

Remote Access VPN: Connects individual endpoints (e.g., remote workers) securely to a private corporate network over the Internet.

Site-to-Site VPN: Connects two entire geographically separated networks (e.g., Branch Office to Main Data Center) using dedicated VPN gateways.
